.TH CONSRV 4
.SH NAME
consrv \- console line server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B consrv
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Consrv
opens
.B /dev/cons
for reading and serves it as one file,
.BR line ,
posted at
.BR /srv/consrv .
It takes no arguments.
.PP
A read of
.B line
answers whatever the console has delivered and not yet been read, and
parks when nothing has: a cooked console delivers a line at a time, so a
read of
.B line
answers what one read of
.B /dev/cons
did.
Held reads do not block the server from answering another client at the
same time.
.B line
cannot be written; a write answers
.BR EPERM .
.PP
The server stops on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the
posted name are both gone.
Every read still held is then answered empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /srv/consrv
the posted service
.TP
.B /line
the console, a line at a time
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/consrv
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR cons (3),
.IR ring (2),
.IR ioproc (2),
.IR thread (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Consrv
exits with a number rather than the empty-string convention other
programs use.
Failing to open
.B /dev/cons
ends it with 116, before it posts anything.
